 "claim": "The reading lemma and the claim rule. (1) The reading lemma (proved). Split a plane knot space at the attachment point: the three faces at the point merge into one face whose boundary is three arcs, one per pair of ports. Along an arc the ports' raw values differ by an arc constant plus the number of skips on it, and the three constants add to 1. So the 'all agree' pattern, which would need a skip on every arc, never occurs, and every port pattern has an odd port k with the merged face's single skip on the arc opposite k. Hence the own arc between ports i and j is barred (carries no skip) exactly when i and j disagree; whenever they agree, the face opposite the third port is claimed by that port's line; and every line of a barred face, other than its claimer, has the two points of its corner there fixed by the port pattern. (2) The claim lemma (proved). A line claims an own arc only in the class of the third port, from one port pattern per claimed value; it claims the face opposite port k only outside class k, from at most two port patterns per value, which agree on the other two ports and differ at k. (3) The claim rule. When a pair of handle points freezes a gluing line (two patterns, two points fixed) and one point of the pair alone excludes the line's claim at either fixed value, the candidate knot has a route and is generated (proved from the route lemma). When each point allows both claims the knot is new: computed with no disagreement against exact creation (728 cases on two 10-point grown spaces with the Fano plane and census 58) and against the pipeline (26,634 lines beyond distance 3 and 3,018 nearer lines on thick tubes, a grid, grown spaces and every point of a ladder arm spliced onto a grid). Nearer lines squeezed in another form (one pattern, or two with one point fixed) are outside the rule; all of them create.",
